  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms are possible through this\nevening, along with a local flash flood risk.\n\nMCV over central PA and cold front approaching from NW PA (and\ngeneral height falls within the upper trough) will serve as\nforcing to continue the development of widespread showers and\nthunderstorms this afternoon. Shear will be sufficient for\nsupercells, but cluster and bowing segments may eventually\ndominate, presenting a risk of damaging winds. Discrete storms\nwill have potential for hail as well. A tornado threat may\nevolve later this afternoon/early evening as a low level jet\npivots into the area and the trough takes on a negative tilt.\nThe axis of storms will likely be closer to the I-95 corridor by\nthis time, where winds could also be backed a little more closer\nto the bay. At the moment, this threat appears a little more\nisolated and uncertain, but discussion was had with SPC that\nsome areas could be upgraded to a Tornado Watch should discrete\nstorm modes dominate. The forcing features listed above could\nalso lead to some upscale growth or training as the storms get\ncloser to the bay. This might lead to a few instances of flash\nflooding, especially from Baltimore to the northeast, as\nprecipitable water values approach 2 inches. A Flood Watch \nremains in effect for this area. Thunderstorms will likely clear\noff to the east by 10 PM, although there may be a few lingering\nshowers, especially along the Alleghenies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few chances for rain through the weekend, but no \n hazardous weather expected between Wednesday and Monday at \nthis time.\n\nThe upper-low over the northeast CONUS will linger through Friday \nbefore finally pulling off to the northeast. As a result, there will \nbe non-zero shower and thunderstorm chances each day, primarily\nareas further north and east. Friday has the greatest chance of\nbeing dry as surface high pressure finally builds in from the \nwest. \n\nA broad upper-level trough will begin to form over the Ohio \nValley over the weekend. The trough may close off into a low,\nwith the trough axis likely remaining to the west into at least\nMonday. Cloud cover & precipitation chances will begin to \nincrease, particularly between Saturday evening and Sunday as \nthe low tracks offshore and towards the northeast. Severe \nprobabilities look low at this time, with a pretty weak wind \nfield aloft.\n\nTemperatures throughout the remainder of the week will be near\nto slightly below normal.",
